{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Problem\nNothing validates, before a reconcile pass mutates a live client Jira, that the mapped\nprojects actually EXIST / are visible to the bridge bot. Two live-only failure modes:\n1. A mapped-but-nonexistent (or decommissioned / permission-lost) project key is\n   syntactically valid, so `_validate_project_key` passes; the failure only surfaces\n   deep in fan-out as an ACLI error. This is exactly the `05b8-4efe-8d58-4c27` incident class.\n2. `resolve_project` (projects_store.py) returns `mapping.legacy_default` VERBATIM,\n   unvalidated. Every unbound/legacy ticket (absent bridge_project) resolves there. The\n   cross-project write guard's allowed set = `read_projects().keys()` and EXCLUDES creates\n   (applier.py `_cross_project_targets`), so a legacy_default pointing at a real-but-wrong\n   (or nonexistent) project drives a mass CREATE storm into the wrong place, unguarded.\n\n## Approach\nAdd a reconcile-pass PREFLIGHT that runs BEFORE any outbound mutation and validates that\nevery project in `read_projects(repo_root).keys()` PLUS `legacy_default` (when set) is\nvisible to the configured backend. The probe reuses `access_check.py`'s existing\n`AcliClient` transport (the same Jira Cloud transport `run_access_check` already drives) and\nits retry helper (`_retry_sleep` / `JQL_RETRY_COUNT`). It enumerates the bot-visible project\nkeys through the client's ALREADY-PRESENT generic REST accessor\n`AcliClient._direct_rest_get` (acli_rest.py:207 — the same helper used by\n`get_issue_property`, `transition_issue`, and `validate_assignee_exists`) against the\nstandard Jira Cloud \"Get projects paginated\" endpoint `GET /rest/api/3/project/search`\n(paginated via `startAt` with the response envelope's `values[].key` / `isLast`). No new\ntransport class or subprocess is introduced — only a new `access_check.py` helper consuming\nthe existing `_direct_rest_get` accessor.\n\nBackend-neutrality: `access_check.py` is already Cloud/ACLI-specific, and this probe is a\nCloud-transport probe. The wiring GATES ON THE CONFIGURED BACKEND: it reads\n`rebar.config.load_config().reconciler.backend` and only builds the Cloud `AcliClient` and\nruns the probe when that key is `\"jira\"` (the Cloud/ACLI backend). For any other backend\n(e.g. `\"jira-datacenter\"`, whose pycontribs transport exposes no `_direct_rest_get` /\n`/rest/api/3` urllib path) the preflight logs an observable \"skipped: backend <key> has no\nvisible-projects probe\" message and returns without building a Cloud client or blocking the\npass. As defence in depth the helper itself also degrades (observable skip) if handed a\nclient lacking a callable `_direct_rest_get`. It only enforces on the Cloud/ACLI transport it\nis built for.\n\nOn a missing/invisible project (transport present, project absent from the visible set), FAIL\nFAST with a clear, actionable message naming the offending key(s) and the fix (correct\nprojects.json / legacy_default), rather than letting fan-out crash mid-pass. A hard\nauth/transport error (the probe cannot reach the backend) fails closed with its own distinct\n\"transport unavailable\" message, kept separate from \"project not visible\". Wire it at the\nreconcile pass entry (`__main__.py` `run_pass_result`, strictly before\n`reconcile.reconcile_once`), skipping read-only passes (dry-run / reconcile-check / preview).\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] A pass with a mapped key not visible to the bot aborts at preflight with a clear\n      message naming the offending key BEFORE any Jira write (no call to\n      `reconcile.reconcile_once`). Verified by\n      `python -m pytest tests/unit/rebar_reconciler/test_preflight_project_visibility.py -q`.\n- [ ] `legacy_default` (when non-empty) is included in the preflight visibility check and an\n      invisible `legacy_default` is caught, naming it. Verified by the same test module.\n- [ ] An all-visible mapping passes preflight and the pass proceeds unchanged\n      (`reconcile.reconcile_once` is called; no happy-path behavior change). Verified by the\n      same test module.\n- [ ] A hard auth/transport error fails closed with its own distinct message\n      (transport-unavailable, not project-not-visible) and the preflight is observable via a\n      log/stderr message. Verified by the same test module.\n- [ ] A non-Cloud configured backend (`config.reconciler.backend != \"jira\"`, e.g.\n      `\"jira-datacenter\"`) is not blocked: the preflight logs an observable skip and the pass\n      proceeds without building a Cloud client. A helper handed a client lacking\n      `_direct_rest_get` likewise skips. Verified by the same test module.\n\n## Testing\n- `python -m pytest tests/unit/rebar_reconciler/test_preflight_project_visibility.py -q` —\n  fake/injected transport (no live Jira): a fake client whose `_direct_rest_get` returns a\n  `project/search` page listing `{GOOD}` visible, `BAD` absent → preflight raises naming\n  `BAD` and the pass makes no mutation; an all-visible mapping passes and the pass proceeds\n  (`reconcile.reconcile_once` called); `legacy_default=WRONG` (not visible) is caught; a\n  `_direct_rest_get` that raises fails closed with the transport-unavailable message; a fake\n  client lacking `_direct_rest_get` is skipped (pass proceeds). RED first: without the\n  preflight the pass proceeds to fan-out.\n- `make lint` and `make typecheck` from the worktree.\n\n## Sequencing\nLives in `access_check.py` (new mapped-projects preflight helper consuming the existing\n`_direct_rest_get` accessor) + wiring at the pass entrypoint (`__main__.py`\n`run_pass_result`). Disjoint from A/B/C/E files. Reads projects_store (no edit)."}},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1786813396311995001,"uuid":"88cf3df8-6dda-4ef9-afaf-304cb35ae9a2"}